5:[["$","$L10","2.8",{"topic":{"id":"2.8","title":"Chain of Custody in Digital Investigations","minutes":13,"summary":"The chain of custody is a continuous, testable account of who controlled an exhibit, what changed, and why.","sections":[{"title":"Start the record at collection","body":["A custody record identifies the case, exhibit, description, source, collector, date and time, location, and relevant device identifiers. It should connect the physical item to its digital acquisitions and derived exports. A laptop, its disk image, and a recovered invoice may need distinct identifiers linked by a documented parent-child relationship.","Record each transfer with sender, recipient, purpose, time, condition, and acknowledgment. Include storage locations and access events under the organization’s procedures. For digital copies, access logs and integrity checks can supplement signed transfer records. A hash alone is not a chain of custody because it says nothing about who possessed the source."]},{"title":"Explain transformations and exceptions","body":["Compression, format conversion, decryption, carving, and export can create new files. Preserve the original acquisition and document each transformation with its tool, settings, input, output, and hash. A report should distinguish the original exhibit from a working copy and a presentation excerpt. Redaction for disclosure should create a separate controlled copy, not overwrite the evidence.","If a seal is opened, record the reason, person, time, and replacement seal. If a hash differs, do not conceal it: identify whether the comparison concerns a container, extracted stream, changed live source, or corrupted copy. Investigate the discrepancy before relying on the affected material. Honest documentation of a problem is more defensible than a fabricated unbroken record."]},{"title":"Custody supports, but does not decide, admissibility","body":["Courts evaluate the reliability of the evidence and the circumstances of its handling. A gap may invite challenge, but the legal consequence depends on the facts and applicable law. Avoid categorical claims that every minor documentation error automatically excludes evidence or that a hash cures every gap.","Prepare a custody appendix that a non-specialist can follow from seizure to courtroom. Link exhibit IDs used in the report, seizure record, laboratory receipt, and certificate. Store personal data securely and restrict unnecessary circulation. The goal is accountable continuity, not merely collecting signatures after the investigation has finished."]}],"practice":{"title":"Trace one invoice through the lab","scenario":"Exhibit L01 is a laptop; I01 is its image; D01 is a recovered invoice exported from I01.","steps":["Create a parent-child exhibit map.","Record the acquisition and transfer to the examiner.","Log the export and its hash separately from the image hash.","Explain which file is being certified and presented."],"debrief":"Distinct identifiers prevent the common mistake of using a laptop’s serial number as if it identified every extracted file and later copy."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["bsa","bnss"],"sources":["cert","bsa","nist-guide"],"labId":"acquisition","unit":2,"objectives":["Explain start the record at collection and apply it to an investigation.","Explain explain transformations and exceptions and apply it to an investigation.","Explain custody supports, but does not decide, admissibility and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-2.8","prompt":"Which information belongs in a transfer entry?","options":["Only the investigator’s initials.","Sender, recipient, time, purpose, exhibit identity, and acknowledgment.","Only the final court verdict.","A password for unrestricted public access."],"answer":1,"explanation":"A transfer entry must make the movement and accountability of the exhibit reconstructable.","unit":2}}}]]
